WEBVTT

00:04.650 --> 00:07.362
This is Allen Company training on caring for and

00:07.362 --> 00:10.713
inspecting your MSA W65. You've seen how to put

00:10.713 --> 00:13.200
it on. Now you'll learn how to keep it ready,

00:13.200 --> 00:16.650
because a W65 you can't trust is no protection

00:16.650 --> 00:17.400
at all.

00:18.503 --> 00:22.841
Know what it does. The W65 is a filter self-rescuer:

00:22.841 --> 00:25.504
it turns carbon monoxide in the air you breathe

00:25.504 --> 00:28.678
into carbon dioxide. It does not make oxygen.

00:28.678 --> 00:31.828
If the air is low in oxygen, it can't help you,

00:31.828 --> 00:34.604
so use it only to get out, by your escapeway,

00:34.604 --> 00:37.391
to fresh air. When you breathe through it in smoke

00:37.391 --> 00:40.803
or carbon monoxide, the air gets hot and dry.

00:40.803 --> 00:43.529
That's normal; it means the unit is working.

00:43.529 --> 00:45.553
Never take it off because of the heat.

00:45.553 --> 00:47.916
Keep your nose clip on and keep breathing through

00:47.916 --> 00:49.928
it until you reach fresh air.

00:51.054 --> 00:53.492
Every shift, before you go underground,

00:53.492 --> 00:57.092
look it over: no dents or deep gouges in the case,

00:57.092 --> 00:59.967
the red lever and its seal intact, the label and

00:59.967 --> 01:02.604
dates readable, and the belt loop secure.

01:03.720 --> 01:06.582
Every 90 days, the unit is weighed to the nearest

01:06.582 --> 01:09.270
gram and compared with the as-shipped weight on

01:09.270 --> 01:11.945
the bottom. A gain of more than 10 grams means

01:11.945 --> 01:15.007
moisture got in, and it's taken out of service.

01:15.007 --> 01:17.757
At Allen, each weigh check is logged in the company's

01:17.757 --> 01:19.295
Dropbox records.

01:20.430 --> 01:23.068
Take it out of service if the dates or weight can't

01:23.068 --> 01:26.343
be read, if it's past fifteen years from manufacture

01:26.343 --> 01:28.930
or ten years in service, if it fails a tightness

01:28.930 --> 01:31.718
test, if the seal on the red lever is broken or

01:31.718 --> 01:35.130
missing, or if it's ever been opened or used.

01:36.252 --> 01:39.339
Treat it with care: don't drop it, throw it,

01:39.339 --> 01:42.289
sit on it or use it as a tool, and keep it away

01:42.289 --> 01:45.939
from heat. At Allen, you're issued your W65 on

01:45.939 --> 01:49.339
a belt, and it must always be within 25 feet of

01:49.339 --> 01:51.977
you underground. Before you go underground,

01:51.977 --> 01:54.477
the safety manager checks you on inspecting and

01:54.477 --> 01:58.252
putting on your W65, hands-on, and records it on

01:58.252 --> 02:02.364
your MSHA new-miner training form, 5000-23.

02:03.486 --> 02:06.974
Check it every shift. It's weighed every 90 days.

02:06.974 --> 02:09.374
Know the five reasons it comes out of service.

02:09.374 --> 02:12.686
Keep it within 25 feet. If you have to use it,

02:12.686 --> 02:15.536
hot air is normal: keep it on until you reach fresh

02:15.536 --> 02:16.198
air.

02:17.328 --> 02:20.490
Remember: your site's plans and procedures always

02:20.490 --> 02:21.990
override this video.
